    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a3eea234_5c0754d3",
        "filename": "libs/androidfw/ResourceTypes.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4592,
      "author": {
        "id": 1043845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-04-08T20:00:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AFAICT there\u0027s no easy way to make a std::basic_stringstream\u003cchar16_t\u003e (std::ctype\u003cchar16_t\u003e doesn\u0027t exist because reasons: http://stackoverflow.com/a/8170580/632035).\n\nThe advantage of moving to stringstream is that we no longer do the error checking ourselves. There is a possible smaller change that would solve the overflow issue (as you mentioned in your CL scope comment), but I prefer to not rely on us catching every error case ourselves.\n\nCase in point, the old error checking is wrong. Any UTF-16 character with the low byte matching [0-9a-f] but the high byte set to anything will match as a valid hex character (the decimal parsing has the same problem).\n\nShouldn\u0027t the cost here be relatively small? The strings we\u0027re operating on are \u003c 10 characters for valid input.\n\nAll that being said, I was sort of on the fence about this approach myself, so if you still prefer the smaller change that leaves error checking as our responsibility, I\u0027ll change it.",
      "parentUuid": "63f82afb_f01006d8",
      "revId": "6950ef6fda6e9de5588ef6d93fcb2bd9174034e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    }
